{:edb/db-before-t 2319 :edb/db-after-t 2320 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535632 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:03:57.974Z" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119491 1100 "Consider the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s$. Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n$\\mathbf T(s) = -\\dfrac{4}{5}\\sin{s}\\,\\mathbf i+\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sin{s}\\, \\mathbf j + \\cos{s}\\,\\mathbf k$." 13194139535632 false] [17592186119491 1100 "Consider the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) = -\\dfrac{4}{5}\\sin{s}\\,\\mathbf i+\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sin{s}\\, \\mathbf j + \\cos{s}\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119491 1145 "Since we are given that $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin s)i + \\frac{d}{ds}(\\frac{3}{5}\\sin s)j + \\frac{d}{ds}(\\cos s)k \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{5}\\cos si + \\frac{3}{5}\\cos sj - \\sin sk \\\\\nT^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= -\\frac{4}{5}\\cos (\\frac{π}{2})i + \\frac{3}{5}\\cos (\\frac{π}{2})j - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2})k \\\\\n&= 0i + 0j - k \\\\\n&=-k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature at the point where\n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{2})‖ = ‖- k‖ = \\sqrt{0^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535632 false] [17592186119491 1145 "Since we are given that $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j-k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖=‖-k‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119492 1100 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\:2\\cos t \\right\\rangle$." 13194139535632 false] [17592186119492 1100 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\:2\\cos t \\right\\rangle.$" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119492 1145 "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t$. Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf \\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2\\cos t,-2\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(2\\cos t)^{2} + (-2\\sin t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= (r^{′}(t))/(‖r^{′}(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\cos t,-2\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(-\\sin t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\sin t,-\\cos t⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (-\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (‖T^{′}(t)‖)/(‖r^{′}(t)‖) = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535632 false] [17592186119492 1145 "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf \\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119493 1100 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(s) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s , \\: -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s, \\: \\sin s \\right\\rangle,\n\\quad 0\\leq s \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119493 1100 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n$\\mathbf r(s) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s, \\: -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s, \\: \\sin s \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0\\leq s \\lt 2\\pi$." 13194139535632 false] [17592186119493 1145 "Let $\\mathbf T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s$. Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\| = \\| \\mathbf{r}''(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(s)$ and $\\mathbf r''(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(s) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{ds}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s), \\frac{d}{ds}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s), \\frac{d}{ds}(\\sin s)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sin s, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sin s, \\cos s⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(s) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{ds}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sin s), \\frac{d}{ds}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sin s), \\frac{d}{ds}(\\cos s)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s,-\\sin s⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(s) &= ‖r^{″}(s)‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s)^{2} + (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s)^{2} + (-\\sin s)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\cos^{2} s + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos^{2} s + \\sin^{2} s} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} s + \\sin^{2} s} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535632 false] [17592186119493 1145 "Let $\\mathbf T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\| = \\| \\mathbf{r}''(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(s)$ and $\\mathbf r''(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535632 true] [17592186119672 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358426 13194139535632 true] [17592186119672 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358433 13194139535632 true] [17592186119672 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358468 13194139535632 true] [17592186119672 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358482 13194139535632 true] [17592186119672 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358489 13194139535632 true] [17592186119673 1020 #edb/ref 17